% Adapted from Lectures/Lecture-16 - Semantic Analysis.pptx (12 slides).
% Introduces the next Strix language extension. Reviewed 2026-10-27.
% Source types and examples follow the current local language specification.
% References for Wasm validation and representations:
% https://webassembly.github.io/spec/core/valid/instructions.html
% https://webassembly.github.io/spec/core/syntax/types.html
% Python annotations: https://docs.python.org/3/library/typing.html

# CSE450 Translation of Programming Languages
## Lecture 16: Semantic Analysis and Type Checking
---
% PowerPoint slide 2. Compiler structure with semantic analysis emphasized.
### Semantic Analysis in the Compiler
[source] @(70, 155) {$note, size=30px} Source text becomes tokens, then an AST. Semantic analysis checks what that tree means.
[lex] {$node} !rect (90, 285) (300, 80) Lexical analysis
[parse] {$node} !rect (490, 285) (300, 80) Syntax analysis
[semantic] {$node, stroke=focus, textcolor=focus} !rect (890, 285) (300, 80) Semantic analysis
[e1] {$edge} !arrow (395, 325) (485, 325)
[e2] {$edge} !arrow (795, 325) (885, 325)
[tokens] @(400, 270) {$note, w=85px, size=21px, align=center, color=id} tokens
[ast] @(800, 270) {$note, w=85px, size=21px, align=center, color=id} AST
...
[checked] @(70, 440) {$note, color=emp} The result is a checked AST with resolved names and source-type information.
...
[backend] @(70, 545) {$note} Code generation uses that information to select instructions and conversions. More elaborate compilers may add intermediate representations and optimization passes.
---
% PowerPoint slide 3.
### A Valid Parse Can Still Describe an Error
[source] @(70, 165) {$code, w=720px, size=27px} ```cpp
// Each has the shape ID = expression;
count = 7;
count = missing + 1;
count = "hello";
```
[context] @(855, 165) {$note, w=345px, color=focus} Assume `count` is declared `int` and `missing` has no declaration. Which assignments are legal?
...
[answer] @(70, 370) {$note, w=1000px, color=emp} Only the first. The others fail name resolution and type compatibility.
...
[questions] @(70, 430) {$note} Semantic questions require context: Is this name visible? What type does it have? Does the operator accept these operands?
...
[calls] @(70, 535) {$note, color=explain} Calls also need:
...
@(70, 585) {$note, color=explain} - a known signature
...
@(70, 635) {$note, color=explain} - the right argument count, and
...
@(70, 685) {$note, color=explain} - compatible argument types.
---
% PowerPoint slide 4. Wasm validates declared machine types.
### WebAssembly Checks Operand Types
[ints] @(70, 165) {$code, w=720px, size=27px} ```wat
;; lhs and rhs are i32 locals
(local.get $lhs)
(local.get $rhs)
(i32.add)
```
[rule] @(855, 170) {$note, w=345px} i32.add consumes two i32 values and produces an i32.
...
[floats] @(70, 390) {$code, w=720px, size=27px} ```wat
;; lhs and rhs are f64 locals
(local.get $lhs)
(local.get $rhs)
(f64.add)
```
[reject] @(855, 390) {$note, w=345px, color=focus} Using i32.add on f64 operands fails Wasm validation before the function executes.
[boundary] @(70, 625) {$note, size=26px, color=emp} Our compiler must also preserve distinctions that Wasm's numeric types do not express.
---
% PowerPoint slide 9. Characters are int values, not a fourth type.
### Extending Strix's Type System
[table] @(70, 160) {$types, colwidths=(19%,21%,60%)} | Source type | Wasm type | Meaning |
|---|---|---|
| `double` | `f64` | 64-bit floating-point number (done!) |
| `int` | `i32` | Signed 32-bit integer |
| `string` | `i32` | Byte offset of NUL-terminated ASCII text |
...
[literals] @(70, 435) {$note} Literal types: `7` and `0x2A` are int. `7.0` is double. `"hello"` is string.
...
[character] @(70, 535) {$note, color=emp} A character literal such as `'A'` is an int with ASCII value 65. There is no separate char type.
[bounds] @(70, 645) {$note, size=25px, color=id} int range: -2147483648 through 2147483647. A minus sign remains a unary operator.
---
% PowerPoint slides 5-6.
### Source Types Determine the Operation
[table] @(70, 160) {$types, size=26px, pad=13px, colwidths=(32%,22%,46%)} | Expression | Source type | Meaning |
|---|---|---|
| `2 + 3` | int | Integer addition: 5 |
| `2.0 + 3.0` | double | Floating-point addition: 5.0 |
| `"ab" + "cd"` | string | Concatenation: "abcd" |
| `"ab" + 'C'` | string | Append one character: "abC" |
...
[system] @(70, 510) {$note} A **type system** specifies the available types, legal operations, result types, and permitted conversions.
...
[pointer] @(70, 620) {$note, size=26px, color=focus} int and string both use i32 in Wasm. Adding two string offsets with i32.add would not concatenate their text.
---
% PowerPoint slides 7-8. Avoid blanket claims about development speed or performance.
### Static and Dynamic Type Checking
[static] @(70, 165) {$note, w=535px, size=31px, color=emp} **Static typing**
[sbody] @(70, 245) {$note, w=535px} Check source operations before execution. Strix variables have declared types. Expression types follow from the program.
[sex] @(70, 420) {$note, w=535px, size=26px, color=id} Examples: Strix, C++, Java, Rust. Type inference can reduce the need for annotations.
...
[dynamic] @(680, 165) {$note, w=520px, size=31px, color=emp} **Dynamic typing**
[dbody] @(680, 245) {$note, w=520px} Operations inspect runtime values and their types. A variable may hold values of different types over time.
[dex] @(680, 420) {$note, w=520px, size=26px, color=id} Examples: Python, JavaScript, Ruby. Optional static tools can check annotations before execution.
...
[assembly] @(70, 565) {$note} Typical machine assembly offers little checking of source-level meanings. Typed Wasm instructions still cannot distinguish an int from a string offset.
[tradeoff] @(70, 655) {$note, color=explain} Static types catch errors early and guide code generation. Runtime checks still have a role.
---
% PowerPoint slides 10-11. Every declaration has an initializer.
### Initializers and Assignments
[setup] @(70, 155) {$code, w=1140px, size=26px} ```cpp
// Inside a function:
int x = 1;
double amount = 0.0;
string word = "";
```
[rule] @(70, 350) {$note} The right-side type must be compatible with the destination. Same-type assignment is allowed, as is int to double promotion.
...
[a] @(70, 465) {$note, w=560px} `x = 'a';`
[aa] @(690, 465) {$note, w=510px, color=emp} Valid: stores the int value 97.
...
[b] @(70, 535) {$note, w=560px} `amount = x;`
[ba] @(690, 535) {$note, w=510px, color=emp} Valid: convert int to double.
...
[c] @(70, 605) {$note, w=560px} `word = 'a';`
[ca] @(690, 605) {$note, w=510px, color=focus} Error: int cannot become string implicitly.
---
### Numeric Operators Use a Common Type
[table] @(70, 160) {$types, size=27px, pad=12px, colwidths=(32%,22%,46%)} | Expression | Result type | Value |
|---|---|---|
| `5 / 2` | int | 2 |
| `5.0 / 2` | double | 2.5 |
| `2 + 0.5` | double | 2.5 |
| `5 % 2` | int | 1 |
...
[common] @(70, 500) {$note, color=emp} For numeric arithmetic: use double if either operand is double. Otherwise, use int.
...
[remainder] @(70, 610) {$note, size=27px} `%` requires two ints. `5.0 % 2` is a type error, even though 5.0 has no fractional part.
---
### Explicit Numeric Conversions
[good] @(70, 165) {$code, w=750px, size=27px} ```cpp
// Inside a function:
double d = 7;       // Implicit promotion
int i = int(-2.9);  // Explicit conversion
double e = double(i);
```
[explain] @(885, 165) {$note, w=315px} `int` to `double` is allowed implicitly. `double` to `int` requires an explicit cast.
...
[value] @(70, 390) {$note, w=750px, color=emp} `int(-2.9)` truncates toward zero, producing -2.
...
[bad] @(70, 480) {$code, w=750px, size=27px} ```cpp
// INVALID declarations:
int i = 2.9;
int j = int("12");
```
[trap] @(885, 410) {$note, w=315px, color=focus} An explicit double-to-int cast traps at runtime for NaN, infinity, or an out-of-range truncated result.
[strings] @(70, 640) {$note} Numeric casts accept numeric operands. string is NOT a numeric conversion target.
---
### Comparisons and Conditions
[source] @(70, 165) {$code, w=760px, size=27px} ```cpp
function Positive(double x) : int {
  int answer = x > 0.0;
  return answer;
}
```
...
[change] @(890, 170) {$note, w=310px, color=emp} With typed Strix, comparisons and ! produce int 0 or 1.
...
[conditions] @(70, 390) {$note} `if` and `while` accept int or double conditions. Zero is false. Other numeric values are true, including 0.5 and NaN.
...
[string] @(70, 500) {$note, color=focus} A string can never be a condition. To test whether text is nonempty, use `#text > 0`.
...
[machine] @(70, 575) {$note, color=id} Numeric comparisons produce i32 in Wasm. A double used as a condition still needs a comparison with zero.
---
% The original expression-tree examples are rebuilt as editable PSL nodes.
### Inferring an Expression's Type
[source] @(70, 155) {$note, size=30px} Given `int x = 3;`, determine the type of `x + 2.5`.
...
[root] {$node} !rect (380, 275) (210, 75) +
[left] {$node} !rect (150, 450) (210, 75) x
[right] {$node} !rect (610, 450) (210, 75) 2.5
[e1] {$edge} !line (435, 350) (255, 450)
[e2] {$edge} !line (535, 350) (715, 450)
...
[explain] @(900, 270) {$note, w=300px, color=explain} Start with the children: variable declarations and literal forms supply their types.
...
!anim left text x : int
!anim right text 2.5 : double
...
!anim root text + : double
!anim root stroke emp
!anim explain text Numeric addition uses the common type double. Record that result type on the parent node.
...
[conversion] @(70, 615) {$note, color=emp} Code generation must promote x to f64 before performing f64.add.
---
### AST Operation and Expression Type
[fields] @(70, 165) {$code, w=750px, size=26px} ```text
AST operation:   Binary
Operator:        +
Left child:      Variable(x), type int
Right child:     Number(2.5), type double
Expression type: double
```
...
[different] @(885, 170) {$note, w=315px} The AST kind tells us which operation to analyze. The source type tells us what value that operation produces.
...
[annotation] @(70, 430) {$note, color=emp} Add source-type information to expressions, variables, and function signatures. Analyze child expressions before their parent.
...
[emit] @(70, 540) {$note} The backend reads the annotations to select integer instructions, floating-point instructions, or string runtime operations.
...
[pass] @(70, 650) {$note, color=id} The semantic pass checks and annotates. It does NOT execute the source program.
---
% PowerPoint slide 12: string + int is legal, but its result is a string.
### Checking an Assignment Tree
[source] @(70, 155) {$note, size=30px} Given `int x = 1;` and `string y = "hi";`, is `x = y + 3;` legal?
[assign] {$node} !rect (430, 260) (190, 65) =
[target] {$node} !rect (190, 400) (190, 65) x : int
[plus] {$node} !rect (670, 400) (190, 65) +
[y] {$node} !rect (520, 545) (200, 65) y : string
[three] {$node} !rect (820, 545) (190, 65) 3 : int
[e1] {$edge} !line (475, 325) (285, 400)
[e2] {$edge} !line (575, 325) (765, 400)
[e3] {$edge} !line (720, 465) (620, 545)
[e4] {$edge} !line (810, 465) (915, 545)
...
[explain] @(940, 260) {$note, w=270px, size=26px, color=explain} First check the right subtree.
...
!anim plus text + : string
!anim plus stroke emp
!anim explain text string + int is legal: append the character with that ASCII code. Its result is string.
...
!anim assign stroke focus
!anim assign text = : error
...
[result] @(70, 650) {$note, color=focus} The assignment fails: a string result cannot be stored in the int variable x.
---
% PowerPoint slides 11-12, updated to current literal and string rules.
### Assignment Practice
[context] @(70, 155) {$note, size=29px} Assume `int x = 1;` and `string y = "hi";`. Check each statement independently.
[a] @(70, 250) {$note, w=500px} `x = x + 2;`
[b] @(70, 325) {$note, w=500px} `y = 'c';`
[c] @(70, 400) {$note, w=500px} `y = x;`
[d] @(70, 475) {$note, w=500px} `y = 'a' + 'b';`
[e] @(70, 550) {$note, w=500px} `y = "" + 'c';`
...
[aa] @(605, 250) {$note, w=595px, color=emp} Valid: int addition, then int assignment.
...
[ba] @(605, 325) {$note, w=595px, color=focus} Error: 'c' is an int.
...
[ca] @(605, 400) {$note, w=595px, color=focus} Error: x is an int.
...
[da] @(605, 475) {$note, w=595px, size=27px, color=focus} Error: character-code addition produces int 195.
...
[ea] @(605, 550) {$note, w=595px, size=27px, color=emp} Valid: appending the character produces "c".
...
[rule] @(70, 620) {$note, color=id} A type rule depends on operand types, not how a programmer might intend to display a value.
---
### Checking Function Calls
[source] @(70, 155) {$code, w=780px, size=25px} ```cpp
function Half(double x) : double {
  return x / 2.0;
}

function Example() : double {
  int count = 7;
  return Half(count);
}
```
...
[signature] @(900, 170) {$note, w=300px, color=explain} Half expects one double argument and returns double.
...
[valid] @(900, 330) {$note, w=300px, color=emp} Half(count) promotes int to double and returns 3.5.
...
[errors] @(70, 500) {$note} `Half()` and `Half(1, 2)` have the wrong argument count. `Half("7")` has an incompatible argument type.
[scope] @(70, 610) {$note} Register a signature before its body to allow self-recursion. Calls can name the current function or an earlier definition. Forward calls are not supported.
---
### An Accepted Conversion Must Generate Code
[wat] @(70, 150) {$code, w=780px, size=25px} ```wat
(module
  (func $half (param $x f64) (result f64)
    (local.get $x)
    (f64.const 2)
    (f64.div))
  (func $example (result f64)
    (local $count i32)
    (i32.const 7)
    (local.set $count)
    (local.get $count)
    (f64.convert_i32_s)
    (call $half))
  (export "Half" (func $half))
  (export "Example" (func $example))
)
```
...
[convert] @(900, 175) {$note, w=300px, color=emp} The argument starts as i32. Convert it to f64 before calling Half.
...
[missing] @(900, 330) {$note, w=300px, color=focus} Accepting the source call in the type checker does not perform this conversion.
...
[other] @(900, 520) {$note, w=300px, color=id} Apply the compatibility rule at initializers, assignments, returns, and each call argument.
---
### A Semantic Visitor
[pseudo] @(70, 160) {$code, w=780px, size=24px} ```text
AnalyzeExpression(node):
    analyze each child expression
    look up this operation's type rule
    reject incompatible operand types
    record and return the result type

CheckValueForDestination(value, expected):
    actual = AnalyzeExpression(value)
    accept actual == expected
    also accept int to double
    otherwise report a source error
```
...
[context] @(900, 175) {$note, w=300px} Resolve variable types from their bindings and call types from function signatures.
...
[checks] @(900, 355) {$note, w=310px, color=explain} Statement visitors also check numeric conditions, assignment targets, loop context, and return requirements.
...
[all] @(70, 590) {$note, color=emp} Check both branches and unreachable statements. Preserve source locations so a type error points to the relevant expression.
---
### Type Errors and Runtime Traps
[static] @(70, 165) {$note, w=535px, size=31px, color=emp} **Rejected during compilation**
[bad] @(70, 250) {$code, w=535px, size=25px} ```cpp
// Separate invalid fragments:
int n = "hi";
double r = 5.0 % 2;
if ("hello") return 1;
```
...
[runtime] @(680, 165) {$note, w=520px, size=31px, color=emp} **Well typed, but may trap**
[good] @(680, 250) {$code, w=520px, size=25px} ```cpp
// Given int n, int i, string s:
10 / n;
s[i];
int(1.0 / 0.0);
```
...
[explain] @(70, 475) {$note} Types alone do not prove that a divisor is nonzero, an index is in bounds, or a numeric conversion is in range.
...
[roles] @(70, 575) {$note, color=explain} Semantic analysis rejects illegal source operations. Generated code and the runtime enforce checks that depend on values.
---
### Questions to Check Your Understanding
...
[a] @(70, 170) {$note, size=30px} Why is Wasm validation insufficient to enforce all Strix type rules?
...
[aa] @(100, 250) {$note, color=emp} `int` and `string` offsets both use i32, but their legal source operations differ.
...
[b] @(70, 355) {$note, size=30px} What is the source type of `2 < 3.0`? What conversion does its comparison need?
...
[ba] @(100, 435) {$note, color=emp} The result is int. Promote the left operand to double, then compare two f64 values.
...
[c] @(70, 540) {$note, size=30px} What information should reach code generation after semantic analysis?
...
[ca] @(100, 620) {$note, w=1100px, color=emp} Resolved bindings and signatures, expression types, and enough context to emit each required conversion.
